ATMENT: initial evaluation captures available patient and staging-relevant information at the first encounter, while acknowledging gaps that may be filled later. TREATMENT then proceeds as the next phase during which additional clinical or staging-related information can be gathered and incorporated as therapy begins and progresses. [Data: Entities (1633, 80); Relationships (2745)]\\n\\nThis linkage implies a workflow where early documentation is explicitly provisional, and subsequent treatment steps can refine or complete the clinical picture. The community frames this as a complementary relationship rather than a strict handoff—treatment and assessment remain interconnected as new data emerges. [Data: Entities (1633, 80); Relationships (2745)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Treatment is the central hub connecting decision factors, settings of care, and modality options\",\"explanation\":\"TREATMENT is the highest-degree entity and the organizing hub for the rest of the community. Its description encompasses a broad set of cancer-directed modalities (e.g., surgery, radiation, systemic therapies, clinical trials) and emphasizes that treatment is selected and personalized using diagnostic and planning inputs such as pathology, imaging, biomarkers, genetic findings, and patient characteristics and preferences. [Data: Entities (80)]\\n\\nThe relationships radiating from TREATMENT connect it to where care is delivered (high-volume/specialty centers), to patient factors that influence selection (age at diagnosis), to common considerations (risk of recurrence, dental work before some therapies), and to treatment attributes and options (combination therapy, aggressive treatment, radiopharmaceuticals, stabilization, and effectiveness). This structure underscores that treatment planning is multi-factorial and iterative. [Data: Entities (80, 2386, 1401, 2606, 813); Relationships (4214, 2285, 4752, 1114, 4255,+more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Specialized/high-volume care settings are explicitly recommended for treatment delivery\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes explicit recommendations to receive treatment at a HIGH-VOLUME CENTER and at a HIGH-VOLUME CENTER OR HOSPITAL when possible, indicating that experience and volume are treated as important contextual factors for care delivery—especially for complex cancer types. [Data: Entities (2386, 1401, 80); Relationships (4214, 2285)]\\n\\nA related specialization is highlighted for brain tumors: the network links TREATMENT to a BRAIN TUMOR CENTER, suggesting that center-specific expertise is considered relevant to treatment selection and delivery for these diseases. Together, these nodes emphasize that the “where” of care is part of the treatment decision landscape. [Data: Entities (5502, 80); Relationships (12247)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Age at diagnosis is a documented driver of treatment decisions\",\"explanation\":\"AGE AT DIAGNOSIS is presented as a patient characteristic that influences treatment decisions, including explicit mention that it affects treatment approaches in acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) and is also a consideration in deciding whether certain therapies may be helpful. The community encodes this as a direct relationship: treatment decisions depend in part on age at diagnosis. [Data: Entities (2606, 80); Relationships (4752)]\\n\\nThis linkage positions age as a formal decision variable within the treatment planning process, alongside other clinical inputs (e.g., subtype and additional factors noted in the broader treatment description). The network’s structure reinforces that treatment selection is not purely disease-based but also patient-context-based. [Data: Entities (2606, 80); Relationships (4752)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Risk of recurrence is an acknowledged baseline consideration across treatment types\",\"explanation\":\"The network explicitly connects TREATMENT to RISK OF RECURRENCE and states that all treatment types have some risk of recurrence. This makes recurrence risk a cross-cutting consideration that persists regardless of chosen modality. [Data: Entities (813, 80); Relationships (1114)]\\n\\nBy including recurrence risk as a linked entity, the community indicates that follow-up monitoring and the expectation of potential return of disease are integral to how treatment is framed and assessed over time. [Data: Entities (813, 80); Relationships (1114)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Pre-treatment dental procedures may be recommended as part of care planning\",\"explanation\":\"The relationship between TREATMENT and DENTAL PROCEDURES indicates that, if dental work is needed, it may be recommended before starting certain cancer treatments. This is a practical planning element that can affect sequencing and readiness to begin therapy. [Data: Entities (2419, 80); Relationships (4255)]\\n\\nThis linkage highlights that treatment planning can require non-oncologic preparatory steps to reduce complications or support safe delivery of therapy. Even though the dataset does not specify which treatments, the community clearly encodes dental readiness as a recognized pre-treatment consideration. [Data: Entities (2419, 80); Relationships (4255)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Treatment options and strategies include combination therapy, aggressive intensity, and radiopharmaceuticals for advanced prostate cancer\",\"explanation\":\"The community connects TREATMENT to COMBINATION OF TREATMENTS, described as a main approach option (e.g., combining chemotherapy, radiation, and/or stem cell transplant). This highlights that multi-modality regimens are a standard strategy in at least some settings captured by the dataset. [Data: Entities (4160, 80); Relationships (8629)]\\n\\nIt also links TREATMENT to AGGRESSIVE TREATMENT, defined as a stronger or more intense approach than other options, indicating that intensity stratification is an explicit dimension of treatment planning. [Data: Entities (5739, 80); Relationships (12935)]\\n\\nAdditionally, RADIOPHARMACEUTICALS are connected